+Each device plays the music in its own player and computes the position from the anchor. A new track, a track change and resuming are anchored ahead by a start lead: 3 seconds for YouTube tracks and 1.5 seconds for audio files. Devices load during this time and start at the same moment from the same position. If the drift exceeds 1.5 seconds, the position is corrected silently. Shared audio files are encrypted uploads like message attachments and are decrypted and played on the device. YouTube tracks play in the official YouTube embedded player inside an isolated frame from the `youtube-nocookie.com` origin. No Google code runs in the app's own origin, and the YouTube API scripts are not loaded. The frame is never loaded before the person gives consent on that device, and the consent can be withdrawn in Settings > Privacy and security.
+
+Audio file tracks play in a single `<audio>` element for the lifetime of the engine, and only the source address changes when the track changes. The interface draws the card right away on the engine's track and state events. While the page is hidden (a background tab, a minimized window) the browser holds back animation frames, so drawing is done with a timer, and the YouTube player area is drawn at least 200x200 in the background too and the track starts together with the other devices. When the card is not visible, the player is moved to one of the four corners and can be minimized. The engine's local hold (a minimized player, a minimized window or a window hidden in the tray reported in the desktop app by `telsizDesktop.onWindowState`, Pause in the system's media controls) only stops the player on that device and does not change the shared state. When the hold is released, the player jumps to the shared position. A YouTube track is not played while its player is not visible, and the minimize and window holds apply only to YouTube tracks.
+
+When a track is added, the client of the person adding it sends an encrypted announcement message to the text room. The `d` field in the plaintext of the message is `{ op: 'add', src: 'yt', vid, title }`, or `{ op: 'add', src: 'file', title }` for an audio file. `title` is optional, the receiver removes control and direction characters from it and shortens it to at most 120 code points, and older clients ignore the field. The name of a YouTube track may not be known when it is added, and the first device that loads its player writes the name into the DJ state. The person adding waits up to 8 seconds, if no name arrives the announcement goes out without it, and once the name arrives the person adding edits their own message. The edited label is not shown on DJ announcements.
 
 If the server owner turns off Telsiz DJ, the server rejects DJ writes. If the YouTube source is turned off, the restriction is applied on the members' devices, since the server cannot see the encrypted state. When restricted mode (`music.restricted`) is on and someone with the DJ permission is in the room, the server rejects writes from people without the permission with `403 dj_restricted`. If nobody with the DJ permission is in the room, everyone can write, so end of track transitions (the clients' automatic writes) continue after the DJ leaves.
 
@@ -224,6 +228,8 @@ Telsiz is designed to hide content from the server, but it does not hide everyth
 
 **YouTube.** When consent is given for a YouTube track, the player loads from Google's servers. Google sees the IP address of that device and which video is played, may collect tracking data and may show ads. The device of a person who does not give consent does not connect to YouTube. No request goes to Google for file tracks. When the server owner turns off the YouTube source, this is enforced on the members' devices. Since the DJ state is encrypted, the server cannot enforce it on the content, and a modified client could still add a YouTube track to the queue.
 
+**Telsiz DJ media controls.** The name of the playing track and the name of the voice room are given to the system's media controls and the lock screen (Media Session). The name comes from the end-to-end encrypted DJ state and so leaves the app, and it is also visible on a locked screen. It can be turned off in Settings > Privacy > Media controls. Play and Pause there only affect the player on that device.
+
 **Telsiz DJ author information.** The "added by" information in the queue is checked against the author records reported by the server. A malicious server could show this information wrongly, but it cannot change the contents of the tracks.
 
 **Availability.** The server can withhold or delay messages, delete accounts and stop the service. End-to-end encryption protects the confidentiality of content, it does not guarantee that the server keeps running.
